# Regulating Electronic Structure of Bimetallic NiFe‐THQ Conductive Metal–Organic Frameworks to Boost Catalytic Activity for Oxygen Evolution Reaction

## abstract
Recently, conductive metal–organic frameworks (c‐MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention for the application in oxygen evolution reaction (OER) due to their superior conductivity and highly accessible active sites. Herein, a facile method is reported to prepare a series of bimetallic c‐MOFs (NixFe1‐x‐THQ, THQ = tetrahydroxy‐1,4‐benzoquinone hydrate) by using the smallest benzene‐based ligand with high‐density catalytic active sites for OER. By adjusting the ratio of Ni/Fe, a series of catalysts with optimal electronic structures and adsorption capacity to intermediates, and faster charge transport are obtained. Among them, Ni0.5Fe0.5‐THQ exhibits superior catalytic performances for OER with a low overpotential of 272 mV at 10 mA cm−2, a small Tafel slope of 47.9 mV, and robust operation for 40 h with no detectable activity decay, which is far superior to commercial RuO2, most of traditional MOFs and even some monometallic conductive MOFs. Moreover, both the measured and simulated results manifest that Ni0.5Fe0.5‐THQ can tailor the d‐band center, and induce the directional transfer of electrons, optimizing the rate‐determining step and adsorption capacity to intermediates during OER, thus exhibits superior electrochemical activity. This work not only presents a strategy to fabricate an electrocatalyst with remarkable OER performance but also promotes the development of THQ‐based c‐MOFs for electrocatalysis.

Recently, conductive metal–organic frameworks (c-MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention for the application in oxygen evolution reaction (OER) due to their superior conductivity and highly accessible active sites. Herein, a facile method is reported to prepare a series of bimetallic c-MOFs $(\mathsf{N i}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{F e}_{1\cdot\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{\overline{{\mathsf{I}}}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{Q},\mathsf{T}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{Q}}=$ tetrahydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone hydrate) by using the smallest benzene-based ligand with high-density catalytic active sites for OER. By adjusting the ratio of Ni/Fe, a series of catalysts with optimal electronic structures and adsorption capacity to intermediates, and faster charge transport are obtained. Among them, ${\sf N i}_{0.5}\mathsf{F e}_{0.5}{\bf-T H Q}$ exhibits superior catalytic performances for OER with a low overpotential of $272~\mathsf{m V}$ at $\mathsf{10m A c m^{-2}}$ , a small Tafel slope of $\mathsf{47.9}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ , and robust operation for $40h$ with no detectable activity decay, which is far superior to commercial $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ , most of traditional MOFs and even some monometallic conductive MOFs. Moreover, both the measured and simulated results manifest that $\mathsf{N i}_{0.5}\mathsf{F e}_{0.5}$ -THQ can tailor the d-band center, and induce the directional transfer of electrons, optimizing the rate-determining step and adsorption capacity to intermediates during OER, thus exhibits superior electrochemical activity. This work not only presents a strategy to fabricate an electrocatalyst with remarkable OER performance but also promotes the development of THQ-based c-MOFs for electrocatalysis.
## 1. Introduction
Water electrolysis is regarded as a sustainable and eco-friendly pathway to produce clean and renewable hydrogen energy.[1]
However, the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) of water electrolysis limits the efficiency of hydrogen generation due to its intrinsically sluggish kinetics, which involves a complex four-electron transfer process.[2] Noble metal-based catalysts like $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ exhibit excellent OER catalytic performance, but the scarcity and high-cost limit their large-scale application in water electrolysis.[3] Therefore, a great deal of efforts have been devoted to developing transition metal-based electrocatalysts, such as transition metal oxides, hydroxides, nitrides, carbides, sulfides, and phosphides, to replace commercial $\mathtt{R u O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ .[ 4 ]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), characterized by large specific surface area, unique and adjustable pore/channel structures, and abundantly accessible metal sites, have been considered as promising alternative materials to commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ for OER.[5] However, due to impeded charge transport caused by their poor conductivity, traditional MOFs are usually carbonized at high temperatures to enhance charge transport when used as OER electrocatalysts. The high-temperature carbonization will dramatically decrease specific surface area and destroy well-defined pore/channel structures of MOFs, which thereby buries a considerable quantity of active metal sites into nanoparticles and finally degrades the OER catalytic performance.[6]
Due to the high delocalization of $\pi$ -electrons caused by conjugation between d-orbitals of metal and $\pi$ -orbitals of ligands with planar rigid structures, conductive MOFs (c-MOFs) possess higher conductivity than traditional MOFs and have begun to show potential prospects as direct catalysts for OER without high-temperature carbonization.[7] c-MOFs based on the redox-active linkers HHTP (2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene) and HITP (2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene) such as $\mathrm{FeCo}_{0.6}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.4}.$ - HHTP,[8] FeCoNi-HHTP/LDH-48,[9] $\mathrm{S/N{\cdot}C M F@F e_{x}C o_{y}N i_{1\mathrm{-}x\mathrm{-}y}}}$ HHTP,[10] ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ ,[11] $\mathrm{Co_{x}Z n_{3-x}(H I T P)_{2}}$ [12] and $\mathrm{La}_{0.6}\mathrm{Sr}_{0.4}\mathrm{Co}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ nanofibers[13] have been demonstrated to be good OER catalysts in alkaline electrolyte with lower overpotential and longer stability compared with commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ . To date, most of the research have focused on the above triphenylene-based ligands.[14] However, compared to the triphenylene-based ligands with large backbone structures, smaller benzene-based ligands with smallest $\pi$ -conjugated backbone such as hexaaminobenzene (HAB) and hexahydroxybenzene (HHB) have been predicted to display higher active site densities and faster carrier transport due to the short distance and good overlap between the electronic wave functions of adjacent ligands and metals,[15] thus have greater potential for electrocatalytic OER applications.[16] As a cheaper oxidation product of HHB with $D_{3\mathrm{h}}$ symmetry,[17] tetrahydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone hydrate (THQ) with a quinone structure has been theoretically predicted as an ideal ligand to prepare c-MOFs with high OER catalytic activity, but it is not verified by experiments until now.[18]
In addition, recent works demonstrate that introducing heterogeneous metal centers can offer a feasible strategy to facilitate the charge transport, optimize the $e_{\mathrm{g}}$ orbitals, and modify the electronic structure, which are critical parameters for efficient OER electrocatalyst.[19] Among the numerous reported multimetal-based electrocatalysts, NiFe-based materials have attracted much attention due to their excellent electrocatalytic performances and low costs.[20] It is generally believed that interactions between proximate Ni and Fe atoms are of crucial importance for regulating the electronic structures around the active sites and improving the OER kinetics. For instance, Zhu and co-workers[21] fabricated a self-supporting Fe-rich Fe(Ni)-MOF nanocomposite electrode, which exhibited excellent OER performance due to the combination of the unique nanostructure of the catalyst and the strong coupling effect between Ni and Fe active sites. Furthermore, Wang et al.[7b] found that when the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ doped Ni-HHTP nanowire array was used for OER, the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ active site would promote the formation of hydrogen bonding between OER intermediates and adjacent layers, thus reducing the free energy of OER process. Therefore, NiFe-based bimetallic c-MOFs may provide a reasonable strategy to regulate the electronic structures and adsorption capacity to intermediates, obtain the optimal OER performance, and study the synergistic effect between multiple metals of the c-MOFs catalyst.
Herein, we put forward a facile method to prepare a kind of bimetallic (Ni, Fe) c-MOF based on THQ ligand with more high-density catalytic active sites for OER. These $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}}$ - THQ display optimal electronic structures, fast charge transport, outstanding activity, and durability for OER. In particular, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ exhibits an overpotential of $272\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mV}}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ and excellent electrochemical durability, which is superior to most of the reported overpotential values for commercial $\mathtt{R u O}_{2}$ , traditional, and monometallic conductive MOFs. The regulation of electronic structure, d-band center, and adsorption/desorption energy of reaction intermediates are investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements and density functional theory (DFT) calculations. This work develops an efficient strategy for designing and regulating advanced catalysts with highly accessible active sites in c-MOFs toward clean energy applications.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Material Synthesis and Structural Characterizations
As shown in Figure 1a, $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ was synthesized by a hydrothermal reaction of THQ with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The element ratio of Ni/Fe in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ was adjusted by the mole fraction of ${\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ and $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ used in synthesis $({\bf x}=0$ , 0.2, 0.5, 0.8 and 1.0), which was confirmed by inductively coupled plasma spectroscopy analysis as well (Table S1, Supporting Information). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the series of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}}$ THQ (Figure 1d: Figure S1, Supporting Information) confirm their successful preparation. As shown in Figure 1d, all $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}}$ - THQ showed similar diffraction patterns with peaks at $8.26^{\circ}$ , $16.55^{\circ}$ , and $25.94^{\circ}$ , which are characteristic for the diffractions of (100), (200), and (300) lattice planes, respectively, suggesting these MOFs are isostructural. The measured XRD pattern of FeTHQ is almost identical to the previously reported literatures,[22] which is well-crystallized and used as an example for structural analysis (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The atom positions, fraction factors, and thermal vibration parameters were refined by convergence and satisfied well the reflection conditions, with $R_{\mathrm{p}}=2.03\%$ , $R_{\mathrm{wp}}=2.56\%$ , and the values are in the acceptable range. The details of the refinement for Fe-THQ are listed in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The variation of the length of $\mathrm{{\bf{M}}}{-}\mathrm{{\boldsymbol{O}}}$ bond in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ is obtained by theoretical calculation (Table S3, Supporting Information). Compared with Fe-THQ, the diffraction peak intensity of other c-MOFs is relatively low (Figure 1d), which illustrates the reduced crystallinity with the increase of Ni. It may be due to the local lattice distortion caused by the difference of $\mathrm{\Delta\M-O}$ coordination bond lengths for Ni and Fe. FT-IR spectra and Raman spectra were conducted to further confirm the successful preparation of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}\mathrm{-}T H Q}$ . The peak at 3496 and $3369\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in THQ, which is the absorption peak caused by hydroxyl vibration,[23] disappears in all of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ (Figure 1e; Figure S4, Supporting Information). It indicates that the metal ion has linked with organic ligands to form MOF. It can be seen that the intensity of adsorption peak at $1623\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in all of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}}$ -THQ decreases compared to THQ, which is owing to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ group in the conjugated ketones by resonance between the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O/C=O}$ and metal ion, demonstrating the existence of metal ion in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{\mathrm{1-x}}}$ -THQ.[23] Raman spectra of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ manifest the elimination of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ at $1649~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in THQ. Besides, the appearance of Ni─O peaks at $561~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and Fe─O peaks at $401\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ confirm, respectively, the efficient coordination between metal and O (Figure 1f; Figure S5, Supporting Information).[24]
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) were exploited in order to examine the crystalline morphology of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ . All $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ show an irregular granular structure with a diameter of $10{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ , which indicates that the change of the element ratio of Ni/Fe have no influence on the crystalline morphology of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}}.$ THQ (Figure 1b; Figure S6, Supporting Information). Energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping confirms the existence of C, O, Fe, and Ni elements with homogeneous distribution in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ. The low magnification transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ is shown in Figure 1c, which further verifies their irregular granular structure with the diameter of ${\approx}10-$ $30\mathrm{nm}$ . Unfortunately, all $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}\mathrm{-}T H Q}$ samples are very sensitive to electron beam irradiation, resulting in massive radiolysis damage. Therefore, after exposuring to the electron beam for a long time, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ sample has been severely damaged, along with the aggregation of metal elements (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The lattice fringes of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ in the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) rapidly disappear within a few seconds, and the HRTEM images cannot be obtained. The standard $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption method was used to investigate the specific surface area and porosity of the series of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ (Figure S8 and Table S4, Supporting Information). The result shows that the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ decreases with the increase of Ni content, and Fe-THQ exhibits the highest specific surface area. The decrease in the BET specific surface area further verified the successful doping of more $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ ions.[25] Besides, the enhanced crystallinity will increase the specific surface area.[26] As a result, the decrease in the BETspecific surface area is related to the reduced crystallinity caused by local lattice distortion in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}\mathrm{-}T H Q}$ (Figure 1d). The electrical conductivity values of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ are summarized in Table S5 (Supporting Information). The results show that Fe-THQ exhibits the best conductivity among $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}}$ -THQ, which may be related to its high crystallinity.
The valence states of the elements in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ were analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The full XPS survey spectra show the existence of Fe, Ni, C, and O elements in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The high-resolution Fe 2p spectra of Fe-THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ, and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ that contained $2\mathtt{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ of Fe are shown in Figure 2a. The peaks at 725.74, 723.17, 712.40, and $710.00\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ , respectively,[27] and the corresponding satellite peaks indicate the antibonding orbit between the Fe atom and O atom, verifying the formation of the ${\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}}\mathsf{O}_{6}$ site.[28] The Ni spectra of Ni-THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ exhibit binding energies of ${\approx}855.88\$ and $874.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, demonstrating the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ oxidation state (Figure 2b),[7b] and the corresponding satellite peaks indicate the antibonding orbit between the Ni atom and O atom, which verifies the formation of the $\mathrm{Ni{-}O_{6}}$ site.[7d] As shown in Figure 2d, the spectra of O 1s can be divided into three fitting peaks at 532.90, 531.59, and $530.73\ \mathrm{eV},$ which are assigned to $_\mathrm{O-H}$ of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O}}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta\M-O}$ of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}\mathrm{-}T H Q}$ , respectively.[20d]
The charge transfer process and electronic interactions were elucidated in terms of the valence electron structure in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}}$ THQ based on XPS results. Here the Fe─O─Ni─O─Fe bonding was used to analyze synergistically electronic effects of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions. Compared with Ni-THQ, the Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ shift to higher binding energies with the increase of ${\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ ratio (Figure 2c), which indicates the decrease of electron density of the Ni atom. The reason may be that part of the electrons of $\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ sites shift to $\mathrm{Fe{\mathrm{-}}O_{6}}$ sites through highly conjugated structure, which changes the electronic structure of $\mathrm{Ni{-}O_{6}}$ sites in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ . As shown in Figure 2e, with a highspin valence electron configuration and corresponding three unpaired electrons in $\pi$ -symmetry $(t_{2\mathrm{g}})$ d-orbitals, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}(t_{2\mathrm{g}}{}^{3}\ensuremath{e_{\mathrm{g}}}^{2})$ can achieve a weaker electron interplay between bridging O, while $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}(t_{2g}^{\Delta_{\mathrm{g}}^{6}}{e_{g}^{\Delta_{\mathrm{g}}^{2}}})}$ can achieve a strong $\mathrm{e^{-}{-}e^{-}}$ repulsion between the bridging O due to its fully occupied $\pi$ -symmetry $(t_{2\mathrm{g}})$ d-orbitals. Therefore, after coupling between $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , the strong $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ – $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ repulsion between ${{0}^{2-}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ will trigger the partial electron transfer from $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and then well modulate the electronic configuration of the Ni site in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}\mathrm{-}T H Q}$ , which is demonstrated by the results of XPS analysis. As a result, the bonding strength between Ni sites and the adsorbed oxygen species can be optimized to satisfy the Sabatier principle. With the decrease of electron density, $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ with low half-full ${\bf e}_{\mathrm{g}}$ orbitals will form appropriate bonding with the adsorbed oxygen species, which is beneficial for promoting catalytic activity and accelerating the reaction rate for OER.[29]
## 2.2. Electrochemical Performances
The electrocatalytic OER performance of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ were investigated in $1.0\ \mathrm{w\KOH}$ solution by comprehensive electrochemical characterization. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of as-synthesized catalysts are given without iR compensation correction (Figure 3a). The overpotential values of Ni-THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}.$ - THQ, and Fe-THQ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (the most important value for a $10\%$ efficient solar water-splitting device) are 374, 288, 272, 309 and $436~\mathrm{mV},$ , respectively. Obviously, the catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ depend strongly on $\mathrm{Fe{-}O_{6}/N i{-}O_{6}}$ ratio and shows the best value for $x$ of 0.5. It is worth noting that the oxidation peaks from $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ (the active intermediate for the OER in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}–T H Q^{[30]}})$ ) obviously shift to higher potential (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The oxidation peak from $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to ${\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}}$ in Ni-THQ is located at 1.40 V. With the addition of Fe, the oxidation peak from $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ shifts toward the more positive potential region to 1.44 V, and the overpotential of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ decreased significantly. With the further addition of Fe, the oxidation peak from $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to ${\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}}$ in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ positively shifts to ${\approx}1.47\mathrm{V}$ , which is about to merge into the OER current. As Fe continues to increase, no oxidation peak is found in the polarization curve of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}–\mathrm{THQ}$ , due to a complete overlap between the oxidation peak $(\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ ) and OER current. It indicates that the oxidation peak is suppressed by the presence of Fe,[31] and thus there exists an optimal Ni/Fe ratio (1:1) to obtain the lowest overpotential in $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}}$ -THQ. In addition, the overpotential value of $272\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ is far lower than most of the reported overpotential values for commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , traditional MOFs, and monometallic conductive MOFs $330{-}550\mathrm{mV})$ , and is comparable to the values based on trimetallic conductive MOF and conductive MOF composites (Figure 3g).[7c,d,8,13,16b,32]
The OER dynamic process of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ was carefully examined by Tafel slope estimation (Figure 3b). $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ shows the lowest Tafel slope of $47.9~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , compared with $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ $(58.4\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ $(56.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}},$ ), Fe-THQ $(98.0\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ) and Ni-THQ $(128.2\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ). It manifests that $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ has the fastest electrocatalysis kinetics for OER among $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\cdot x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ.}}$ [33] In order to further understand the electrocatalysis kinetics in the OER process, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was evaluated. $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ exhibits the smallest charge transfer resistance as well (Figure S11, Supporting Information). To further investigate the influence factors for OER activity, the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of each $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ is evaluated according to doublelayer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ estimated from a cyclic voltammetry (CV) test (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Among $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ exhibits the largest $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value of $0.852~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ which is much higher than that of Ni-THQ $(0.385~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}$ -THQ $(0.714\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ), $\mathrm{Ni_{0.2}F e_{0.8}^{\mathrm{}}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ\(0.659mFcm^{-2})}}$ Fe-THQ $(0.552\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -HHTP $(0.378\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ catalysts (Figure 3d; Figure S13, Supporting Information). As expected, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ catalyst possesses the largest ECSA of $21.25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (normalized per $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ of electrode area), which is much higher than that of Ni-THQ $(9.63~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ , $\mathrm{Ni_{0.8}F e_{0.2}}$ -THQ $(17.85~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}–\mathrm{THQ}$ $(16.48~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ , Fe-THQ $(13.80~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{HHTP}$ $(9.45~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ catalysts. The higher ECSA of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ than $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{HHTP}$ indicated its higher active site densities.[34] Importantly, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ catalyst still exhibits the highest intrinsic catalytic activity which is obtained by normalizing each sample with the ECSA (Figure 3d). It indicates that the intrinsic catalytic activity can be promoted by modulating the electron density of metal coordination centers with the second metal ions, which is beneficial to the OER process. It can provide a reference possibility for designing the catalyst in the future. The mass activity of the catalyst was calculated based on the total deposited amount to evaluate its intrinsic activity. As shown in Figure 3e, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ exhibits the highest mass activity of $175\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ at the overpotential of $300\mathrm{mV}$ as compared with those of Ni-THQ $(22.9\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1})}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}.$ THQ $(102.1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}.$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}–\mathrm{THQ}$ $(48.2\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1}},$ ) and FeTHQ $(0.59\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1})}$ . In addition, the intrinsic catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{-THQ}}$ was further evidenced by evaluating the turnover frequency (TOF) value on the assumption that all metal atoms in catalysts are active. Expectedly, $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ shows the highest TOF value of $0.026\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at the overpotential of $300~\mathrm{mV}$ as compared with those of Ni-THQ $(0.00316\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}).$ , $\mathrm{Ni_{0.8}F e_{0.2}}$ -THQ $(0.015\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.2}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}$ -THQ $(0.0073\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , Fe-THQ $(0.000081\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ) and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{HHTP}$ $(0.00028\ \mathrm{s^{-1}})$ (Figure 3e; Figure S14, Supporting Information). The above results illustrate that the enhanced intrinsic catalytic activity, more available active sites, and expedited electrochemical kinetics of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ endow its superior OER performance. The durability test of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}.$ THQ was studied by accelerated degradation measurements conducted chronoamperometry under constant voltage $_{1.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE). There is almost no fluctuation during the $40\mathrm{~h~}$ test, indicating that $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ has excellent electrochemical durability (Figure 3f). In order to further investigate the electrochemical durability of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ during the OER process, its valence states of the elements after OER test were analyzed by XPS. $\mathtt{N i}2\mathtt{p}$ and Fe 2p XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ confirm that the atoms on the catalyst surface maintained their original state (Figure S15, Supporting Information). In addition, the SEM image of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}–\mathrm{THQ}$ indicates that the morphology of $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}.$ THQ has no significant difference before and after electrochemical measurements (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The remarkable electrochemical durability can be ascribed to the strong chelating effect of hexadenticity and coordination bonds between Ni, Fe, and THQ.
## 2.3. DFT Calculations
But it’s worth noting that $\Delta\mathsf{G}_{3}$ of NiFe-THQ (Fe-site) is $0.247\mathrm{eV},$ which is obviously lower that of Ni-THQ $(\Delta\mathrm{G}_{3}=0.368~\mathrm{eV})$ , FeTHQ $(\Delta\mathrm{G}_{3}=0.458~\mathrm{eV})$ ), and the $\Delta\mathsf{G}_{2}$ NiFe-THQ (Ni-site), indicating that the synergistic effect between Fe and Ni in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}-$ THQ can remarkably enhance the OER activity by reducing the energy barrier of intermediate product.
Finally, in order to understand the mechanism of bimetal adsorption enhancement of Fe site in $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}$ -THQ, 3D differential charge density image was calculated to study the electron transfer in the c-MOF. As shown in Figure 4f, the depletion of electrons in the TM─O bond axis direction at both the Fe and Ni sites can be attributed to the shift of electron pairs toward the electronegative oxygen atoms in the $\sigma$ bond. Interestingly, in the $z$ -axis direction perpendicular to the $\sigma$ bond, it can be clearly seen the accumulation of charge around the Fe sites and the depletion of electrons at the Ni sites. This is because the Ni site with more d-electrons can transfer part of its electrons to the Fe site through the $\mathrm{d}{-}\pi$ conjugation on the surface of the material.[29] As a result, the center of the d band of the Fe sites moves toward the Fermi level, showing stronger adsorption of the intermediate, and finally enhancing the intrinsic activity of OER, which is consistent with the results of XPS analysis.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, a feasible method is proposed to develop $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1\mathrm{-}x}}$ - THQ OER catalysts with more high-density catalytic active sites. XPS and DFT results have demonstrated that $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{1-x}\mathrm{\cdotTHQ}}$ can efficiently modulate the electronic structure, thus facilitating electron transport and optimizing the adsorbed free energy of intermediates during the OER electrocatalysis process. In addition, the optimal $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}{\cdot}\mathrm{THQ}$ exhibits more outstanding OER activity and stability in alkaline electrolytes than commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , most of the traditional MOFs, and even some monometallic conductive MOFs. This work puts forward a route to construct bimetallic c-MOFs electrocatalysts with more highdensity catalytic active sites toward highly efficient water splitting and a new strategy of MOF electrocatalyst design and investigation.
